WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.330 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  hey everyone its Peter Brown here to  talk about Zelda breath of the wild and  our full review will not be here until  March 2nd next Thursday than the  meantime here are impressions of the  first five hours of the game if the  entirety of breath of the wild maintains  the beauty and variety seen in its  opening hours it will be a strong  contender for the best Zelda game of all  time and I say this fully aware that any  new Zelda game comes with a hefty dose  of hype and anticipation throw in a  console launch on top of that and you  wouldn't be blamed for sounding the  hyperbole alarm however my enjoyment  isn't tied to holding a shiny new piece  of plastic or simply swinging a sword as  link breath of the wild is an exciting  game for it's incredible landscapes and  how with minimal instruction you're  allowed to find your footing as you go  you have to contend with a dense rule  set in which nearly every decision you  make must be cross-referenced with your  surroundings and because of this  demanding yet satisfying atmosphere you  feel like you're constantly learning  often through failure and death survival  let alone progress is a major concern  you have to understand your physical  limitations wander too far up a snowy  mountain without the proper gear and you  will freeze to death in short order if  you dive into a lake you better pray you  have enough stamina to make it to the  other side you can also scale cliffs now  but you may find yourself stuck mid  climb if the weather takes a sudden turn  for the worse  your ability to get a foothold is  drastically reduced in the rain enemies  of course are another chief concern  rushing into battle is a non option  against anything other than a single  enemy to face a squad of 5 enemies and  not die all it takes is a few hits you  will have to make the most of your  surroundings if you see a red barrel  find a way to set it ablaze  or perhaps wait for nightfall so you can  sneak up on enemies and kill them while  they sleep experimenting and learning  new tactics will get you far but you  also need to be active and aware when  clashing swords or Spears link is  capable of pairing an incoming attack  with the press of a button at the right  time in a brief window forcing you to  pay attention to every move your enemy  you can also dodge and backflip away  from an attack which will leave an enemy  vulnerable if they wish enemies with  pull arms or spears present a different  type of challenge how do you get in  close with a considerably shorter weapon  in hand well by jumping over their  weapon and attacking from overhead get  another maneuver you have to learn on  your own  where battles are a tough nut to crack  at first in a short span of time you  learn how to overcome these obstacles  through necessity and experimentation a  process that is consistently rewarding  despite the number of deaths you incur  along the way every weapon in the game  thus far is acquired by defeating  enemies you therefore come across a lot  of potential weapons both in number and  variety you need to be mindful of their  durability which is only detailed in  words ambiguous weapon durability is the  one shortcoming of breath of the wild  thus far most of the swords spears clubs  and shields found in the early hours of  the game break after just a few fights  it isn't always easy to reconcile  preserving a cool weapon versus fighting  with something that's more common yet  weak but you don't always have a choice  it will be interesting to see how this  plays out in the long term the hope is  that you find more durable weapons that  can have a permanent spot in your  inventory but that remains to be seen at  the moment beyond satisfying your  curiosity exploration is driven by  shrines puzzle driven dungeons littered  across the world they are bite-sized  lasting only a few minutes apiece but  they deliver a completely different tone  compared to the outside world and at the  start of some shrines you'll be granted  a new power known as a rune these  include bombs that can be generated  resource free under a cooldown timer but  you also gain the ability to summon  pillars of ice from bodies of water and  move metallic objects by controlling a  magic magnetic orb tethered to your body  but each shrine is guaranteed to net you  a spirit orb and these act as a currency  of sorts that you can trade in by  praying at a statue to upgrade your  health or stamina meters so far picking  one over the other has been the hardest  decision to make and breadth of the wild  shrines are common but not so common  that you can expect to quickly balance  out your stats with new spirit orbs this  all sounds like a lot to manage and it  is but damn if it doesn't feel good to  play a Zelda game with so many variables  and opportunities available from the  start all this is to say nothing of the  light-hearted events that populate the  world and how they brighten up a dreary  trip through rain and fog after a  hard-fought battle people are affable  and cheeky and animals prove to be a  soothing and beneficial distraction  nuzzle a dog with your face and become  instant  friends tame a wild horse and make it  your own or swipe at a chicken to make a  drop an egg that you can use in your  next meal the stories being shared among  those playing breath of the wild at  GameSpot are all vastly different and  exciting even though we're playing the  same game we're all jealous of one  another breath of the wild embodies the  freedom and danger that made the first  Zelda game so enthralling but it also  captures the feeling of awe that came  when ocarina of time hit the scene in  this case by layering untold variables  into seemingly every facet of the  experience based on our early  impressions it's safe to say that breath  of the wild will forever change what  people expect from the future of the  Zelda series  [Music]

